WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.210
Speaker 1: It's great to have back on the
program today, Brian Klaas, who's an associate

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:08.970
professor of global politics at University
College London and also author of the new

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.440
book Corruptible Who Gets Power and How It
Changes US.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.930
Bryan, always great to talk to you.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.380
Speaker 4: Thanks for having me.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.330
Speaker 1: So, you know, when we think about
something like who obtains power in in society,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:24.910
we could even narrow it down and say, in kind
of western democracies, in capitalist western

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.800
democracies, there's a lot of different approaches
we can look at.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.669
We can look at sort of psychological factors
and personality factors about individuals.

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:37.660
We can talk about, you know, the leg up that
the economic circumstances that one is born

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.329
into might play as a factor.

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:42.879
We could talk about the role of media.

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:44.600
So many different things.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.929
What are some of the or maybe the most important
filters through which we can start to think

00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:52.670
about who ends up holding power in society?

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.799
Speaker 4: Yes.

00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:59.889
So I think that the most of the debate around
people in power is like a tip of the iceberg

00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:04.449
problem where we tend to focus almost all
of our attention on the people who are actually

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:08.430
in power, the ones who we can see right at
the top of the iceberg.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.060
And the real problem I think we have is underneath
the surface, which is the people who don't

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.470
seek power.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.950
And so, you know, you think about like a high
school basketball team, you never expect that

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.939
basketball team to be a representative sample
of height in the school.

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:24.829
There's a certain group of people that are
going to try out for the team.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:27.130
The same is obviously true with power.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.340
Now I've looked, you know, the book draws
on a wide variety of research, from evolutionary

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.090
biology to neuroscience and psychology and
so on.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.439
So I started to ask people who study this
Is there a power gene?

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:41.840
Is there something in us that causes us to
seek power?

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:48.229
And there is a study that shows a significant
correlation between a specific gene and people

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:49.999
who end up in power.

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:52.499
But we can't say whether that's because they
have certain traits.

00:01:52.499 --> 00:01:54.280
They're more outgoing, they're more affable,
whatever.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:00.090
But we do have some evidence that there is
some genetic basis to power attainment.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.899
And then of course, you filter that through
all the stuff in society where, you know,

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:10.490
I mean, it's obvious that white men are have
an easier time getting power in Western democracies,

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:14.820
but also that we seek certain things in our
rulers.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.171
And this ranges from everything from evolutionary
psychology where we look at, you know, why

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:22.800
is it that we turn to strongman in times of
crises?

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.000
To this amazing study, I found about basically
asking children to pick someone to captain

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
their imaginary ship and this computer simulation?

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.840
And they basically are shown two faces, and
there's no other information given they pick

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.930
one of the faces to be the captain.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:43.410
What they don't know is that the two faces
are the winner and the runner up of the French

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.550
parliamentary elections.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:51.090
And with astonishing accuracy, they tend to
pick the winner, which reflects this horrible

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.180
truth that often we have such superficial
assessments when we pick leaders.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.580
So there are dozens of reasons why this happens.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.500
But I think we have to acknowledge the problem
and the self-selection that goes into power

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.840
before we can actually fix it.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.570
Speaker 1: With some of these things, there
would be sort of a legitimate question to

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.460
ask about.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:14.610
It's sort of like a chicken egg thing, and
I'll explain what I mean when we think about

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:19.560
the alleged corrupting influence of power.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.980
It's often said that absolute power corrupts.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:29.050
But is there something about corrupt people
who are already corrupt predisposed to put

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.610
themselves in those positions that kind of
reverses the causation?

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:36.250
Speaker 4: Yes, I'll answer it in two ways.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.250
The first is that there's this.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:43.340
There's this other study that I found phenomenal
where it basically asks people to roll the

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.150
dice, and it says if you roll a six, you get
more money.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:47.720
If you roll a one, you get no money, right?

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.600
So there's an incentive to lie about it.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.440
So they, you know, there's variations of how
many people cheat in this, basically.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.710
But what they found was that when they ran
this study in corrupt countries like in India,

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:01.180
where being a civil servant is somewhere where
you can get off the books pay.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.180
Yeah.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.330
What happened was the people who lied about
their dice rolls were disproportionately likely

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.940
to want to become civil servants.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.650
They did this at a school.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.690
If you do the exact same study in somewhere
like Denmark, it's exactly the opposite.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:17.820
The people who report their stores, their
scores completely, honestly, they're the ones

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.510
who want to go into public service.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.510
So a corrupt system draws in corrupt people,
and a clean system draws in clean people.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:24.970
So the system really, really matters.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:28.690
I talk about this actually in an essay I have
out in the Atlantic today with policing in

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.150
the United States.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.160
So there's this there's this phenomenon that
I think doesn't get enough attention when

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.220
we talk about body cameras and trying to change
what the police do with police reforms, and

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.310
we have to think about who the police are
and who wants to become a police officer.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:46.690
So, you know, I looked at this ad that was
used for recruitment in Doraville, Georgia.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:51.220
There's this town of 10000 people outside
Atlanta, Atlanta, and it's I mean, it's a

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.220
caricature, right?

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:56.530
It's literally like these guys in a tank driving
through in the sort of, you know, the Punisher

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.760
logo is flashing on screen about vigilante
justice that.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.160
Ross, smoke grenades out, that's you know,
that's the image of policing.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:07.950
I talked to New Zealand's police force and
they basically tried to counteract this, so

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:14.170
they ran a multi-year recruitment scheme that
use funny videos, made policing look enjoyable.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:18.320
All about public service had lots of women
and ethnic minorities in it, and their applications

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.690
shot through the roof for non through the
roof for nontraditional officers and their

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.150
abuse levels fell at the same time.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:30.330
So, you know, I think that there is an acknowledgment
we have to make that the way we perceive systems

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.940
of power really, really matters for who seeks
it.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.040
And it's why I'm particularly worried about
what's happening in the US right now with

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.720
these insane zealots threatening school board
members for, you know, mask mandates and so

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:43.720
on.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.190
Yeah, because they're making public service
toxic and they're going to ensure that good,

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.710
decent people just simply self-select out
of systems of power and that we're left with

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:53.940
the people who the insane zealots basically
agree with

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.610
Speaker 1: putting their hat in the ring,
right?

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:56.720
You have that issue.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.810
You also have I mean, it's not necessarily
related to what we're talking about here,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:05.700
but I've pointed out that those who want to
hurt public education funded through taxes

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:11.390
love the school board stuff because they see
it as, Hey, if if these schools seem really

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:16.050
chaotic, it might encourage parents to say,
I'm pulling my kid out, I'm going to do home

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.139
schooling, private school, whatever.

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:21.510
Which will, of course, further weaken schools
and sort of sort of exacerbate.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.310
Let's go back a little bit to the police thing,
because that's interesting.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:28.050
And you've talked about that there recently.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.550
Two stories on policing have sort of come
together.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:37.000
One is what was sort of increased by the George
Floyd protests and questions of police brutality

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.889
and bad apples that spoil the that spoil the
bunch, et cetera.

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:46.750
We also have the anti-vaccine stuff which
is leading to it really doesn't seem like

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.810
more than one percent in the worst cases of
officers leaving, you know, 34 officers in

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.510
New York City out of 36000.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:57.430
A few emails that came in, people said, Hey,
you know, when we talk about the bad apples

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:01.140
that spoil the bunch on the police brutality
stuff, it might be the same people who are

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.690
now leaving because of the vaccine stuff.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.919
This could be a blessing in disguise.

00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:09.490
But that's not really a strategy for dealing
with this issue of policing.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:10.850
What is your sense of?

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:12.310
You know, there's the reform movement.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:17.520
There is the Defund movement, there's all
these different ideas and approaches.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.560
You mentioned one in New Zealand.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.310
That was a very different approach.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:25.770
How should we be thinking about Big Picture,
the issue of policing in the United States

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.180
and who is doing the policing?

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.290
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:31.470
So I mean, the stuff I talked about in New
Zealand is a first step, which is to say you

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:35.830
need to make the police appealing to normal
decent people who want to serve the public

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.360
that don't have a military background.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.360
Right?

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:42.080
Veterans are overrepresented in the police
dramatically in the US by design, by the way.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:43.720
And that's not necessarily a problem.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.930
It's just that, you know, if you think that
your job is the same as when you were patrolling

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.800
in Fallujah, when you're in Ferguson, that's
going to be an issue.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.520
You're going to be more likely to be a little
trigger happy and so on.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.020
And we need a lot more community officers
that have no background in the military and

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:58.250
actually don't want power.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:59.889
So the recruitment is part of it.

00:07:59.889 --> 00:08:03.610
There's other stuff I write about in the book
that's about how you sort of deter abuses

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:05.790
for people once they're in power.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:09.080
You know, I think it is easier to recruit
good apples than it is to make bad apples

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:10.139
behave better.

00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:11.360
But we do have bad apples in the police.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.770
So what are we going to do?

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:19.300
One of the keys is rotation, so there's a
lot of studies that look at how comfort and

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.729
sort of closed systems create abuse.

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:22.729
Hmm.

00:08:22.729 --> 00:08:26.850
So in police, for example, this is the sort
of blue wall of silence and the sort of your

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:30.270
partner will always have your back, even if
you're doing something really illegal and

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.270
abusive.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:35.899
So the Metropolitan Police in London, for
example, did some work of rotating partners

00:08:35.899 --> 00:08:38.969
through regularly, and it had really good
results.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:43.819
And there's also loads of psychological research
where you have participants play games basically

00:08:43.819 --> 00:08:47.420
in which they can collude in order to win
money.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.180
And if you work with the same person through
multiple rounds of the game, the odds that

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:55.699
you're going to try to give bribes to win
the game go massively through the roof compared

00:08:55.699 --> 00:08:59.540
to if you're constantly rotating people through
because you have, you know, this sort of fresh

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.540
face that you don't know is necessarily on
your side.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:07.269
And interestingly, this data shows the same
thing whether they run it in Germany or China

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:08.269
or the U.S..

00:09:08.269 --> 00:09:12.800
So, you know, I have at the end of the book,
there's this section on 10 ways to fix the

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:13.880
problem.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:18.259
And they're basically things that all need
to happen because there's no silver bullet.

00:09:18.259 --> 00:09:20.070
This is a massive problem.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:24.329
But the point that I'm trying to make here
is that we don't think about systems of power

00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:25.519
enough this way.

00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:30.139
We have to design every single system from
the police to politics with the assumption

00:09:30.139 --> 00:09:31.139
which is true.

00:09:31.139 --> 00:09:35.550
By the way, the bad people are disproportionately
likely to seek power, disproportionately likely

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.819
to get it and disproportionately likely to
be able to stay in it longer than they should.

00:09:39.819 --> 00:09:44.910
And so once you take that as the starting
point, then you redesign the system and think,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.430
how can we invert that?

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:48.240
And I think nothing has been tried.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.940
I mean, that's the thing that I find so maddening
is that all of the debates around police.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.520
I mean, there's some defund movements and
so on that have their ideas about, you know,

00:09:58.520 --> 00:09:59.520
fundamentally transforming.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.029
Policing, but the rest of them, body camera,
I mean, it's a good idea.

00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:05.559
But is that really going to solve the problem?

00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:08.980
There's a thousand people who are getting
killed by cops in the United States every

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:09.980
year.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:13.600
In New Zealand, there's about a dozen a decade,
maybe less than that.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.749
So obviously the population is different,
but we have a massive, massive problem on

00:10:17.749 --> 00:10:21.220
our hands and I don't think around the edges
reforms are going to solve it.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:26.410
Speaker 1: What are the different fields in
which these dynamics are a problem?

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:29.689
And I think police is one that gets a lot
of attention.

00:10:29.689 --> 00:10:34.350
Politicians and, you know, psychopathy predispositions
is sometimes talked about.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:37.670
I think corporate CEOs is one that gets discussion.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:43.199
We don't hear about this dynamic among postal
workers or nurses, for example.

00:10:43.199 --> 00:10:47.110
So like, what are the other fields where these
dynamics show up more?

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.639
Speaker 4: Well, it's it's very interesting
you ask that, because there's there's two

00:10:50.639 --> 00:10:54.750
sections in the book where I try to bring
this dynamic much closer to home.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:59.850
And there's one which is a really crazy story
about this guy named Steve Rucci, who is a

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:04.829
psychopathic custodian, interconnected in
New York and in order to raise rise through

00:11:04.829 --> 00:11:08.379
the ranks in his school district, did some
crazy stuff.

00:11:08.379 --> 00:11:14.069
I mean, he started using explosives and all
these years in jail for a very long time now.

00:11:14.069 --> 00:11:17.839
And that job, that was just his personality
traits so that if you have a psychopath who's

00:11:17.839 --> 00:11:21.579
power hungry, you put them in anywhere and
they're going to try to rise through the ranks

00:11:21.579 --> 00:11:23.310
through any means necessary.

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:27.420
But there's actually, as you say, a trait
to this that is disproportionately selective

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:28.420
in certain jobs.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:30.279
So politics is an obvious one.

00:11:30.279 --> 00:11:34.309
But also one that's really interesting is
a place where you have power for the sake

00:11:34.309 --> 00:11:36.899
of power with no real other benefits.

00:11:36.899 --> 00:11:39.209
So I explore this with homeowners associations.

00:11:39.209 --> 00:11:45.370
There's a great, a great series of stories
that I've discovered and characters that are

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:50.029
very interesting people who basically, you
know, it's like this horrible job where you

00:11:50.029 --> 00:11:55.470
spend your time trying to impose fines on
somebody down the block who put their been

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.939
out, you know, 10 minutes too early or something
on a Sunday.

00:11:58.939 --> 00:12:02.339
Who's that going to appeal to when the pay
is either nonexistent or terrible?

00:12:02.339 --> 00:12:05.319
Well, it's the person who's power hungry and
can't fulfill that.

00:12:05.319 --> 00:12:06.319
In another area of life.

00:12:06.319 --> 00:12:11.720
So those types of positions are also magnets
for power abusers, which is why you get what

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:13.730
we call the petty tyrants effect.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:18.889
The person who rules over a completely insignificant
domain but is going to abuse every possible

00:12:18.889 --> 00:12:20.100
ounce of power that they have.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:23.269
And I think that's something that a lot of
people experience in their daily lives

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:30.129
Speaker 1: as a as a general rule are the
approaches that are more likely or have worked

00:12:30.129 --> 00:12:35.259
more effectively about, as you mentioned with
police, it's higher good apples rather than

00:12:35.259 --> 00:12:36.939
convert it.

00:12:36.939 --> 00:12:38.499
Does that apply universally?

00:12:38.499 --> 00:12:40.569
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:12:40.569 --> 00:12:44.989
You sort of need to do all of the above, to
be honest, because the problem is not just

00:12:44.989 --> 00:12:46.839
the individual, it's also the system, right?

00:12:46.839 --> 00:12:50.860
I mean, if you have a good person who goes
into a rotten police department, they're going

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:53.179
to probably behave in a rotten way.

00:12:53.179 --> 00:12:57.680
And one of the more unusual things that I
did that might be of interest here, your viewers

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:03.480
and listeners is I took a ski lesson with
Paul Bremer, who ran Iraq in 2003, and now

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.509
he's a ski instructor in Vermont or not.

00:13:06.509 --> 00:13:11.319
So he's an interesting case study because,
you know, a lot of people see him as the villain,

00:13:11.319 --> 00:13:14.540
and I understand completely why I mean what
he did in 2003.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:16.880
You know, the record speaks for itself.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:21.829
But what's interesting about him is that he
was previously an ambassador to Malawi and

00:13:21.829 --> 00:13:24.819
Norway, and it appears that he did a very
good job.

00:13:24.819 --> 00:13:30.499
I mean, across most people think that he was
really effective and sort of had lots of integrity

00:13:30.499 --> 00:13:32.579
in that position and so on.

00:13:32.579 --> 00:13:36.389
And then he goes into Iraq, where everything
is broken and he's basically, you know, a

00:13:36.389 --> 00:13:37.862
de facto viceroy of a dictatorship.

00:13:37.862 --> 00:13:45.309
And all of a sudden, everything he has to
do is these awful decisions with morally dubious

00:13:45.309 --> 00:13:46.440
consequences and so on.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:50.300
And so I think one of the things that we also
have to acknowledge, it's not to absolve people

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:54.259
who do bad things in positions of power, it's
that it's more complicated sometimes than

00:13:54.259 --> 00:14:00.110
it initially appears and that the system also
needs our attention because, you know, I like

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.639
to think that I would behave well no matter
what.

00:14:02.639 --> 00:14:07.060
But you know, you see the evidence from around
the world and you take people and put them

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:09.069
in new systems and they do behave in different
ways.

00:14:09.069 --> 00:14:14.739
There's a study of bankers in Italy where
southern Italy is known as being more corrupt

00:14:14.739 --> 00:14:15.739
in northern Italy.

00:14:15.739 --> 00:14:16.739
Yes.

00:14:16.739 --> 00:14:20.370
And the employees who move between the same
bank but different branches start embezzling

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:22.550
at higher rates if they're in the south.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:26.369
So, you know, there's things like that where
we can see that it's both the individual and

00:14:26.369 --> 00:14:27.470
the system that need our attention.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:33.559
Speaker 1: This may seem like a sort of divergence,
but but I think it could be an interesting

00:14:33.559 --> 00:14:34.559
one.

00:14:34.559 --> 00:14:39.559
The role of power within other species within
the animal kingdom.

00:14:39.559 --> 00:14:42.809
What do we know that mirrors Homo sapiens
and what?

00:14:42.809 --> 00:14:43.839
What differences are there?

00:14:43.839 --> 00:14:45.809
Speaker 4: Yes, a great question.

00:14:45.809 --> 00:14:48.699
It's one of the most enjoyable parts of the
research because, you know, I'm a political

00:14:48.699 --> 00:14:53.019
scientist, so I don't spend a lot of time
with people who study hyenas and bees and

00:14:53.019 --> 00:14:54.019
primates and so on.

00:14:54.019 --> 00:14:55.440
But I did for the book.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:58.999
Yeah, and it was really, really fascinating.

00:14:58.999 --> 00:15:04.959
Primates are a great window for us because
it's similar in terms of some of the brain

00:15:04.959 --> 00:15:08.389
functions and some social structures without
the culture.

00:15:08.389 --> 00:15:09.389
Right.

00:15:09.389 --> 00:15:13.760
And so you can look at how power affects individuals
is an amazing study.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:21.499
I interviewed a professor and doctor out in
North Carolina who looks at hierarchy and

00:15:21.499 --> 00:15:23.800
drug addiction in macaque monkeys.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:24.800
Hmm.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:29.569
And one of the craziest things is that when
you basically give monkeys, you put them in

00:15:29.569 --> 00:15:30.569
groups of four.

00:15:30.569 --> 00:15:35.980
They instantly form a hierarchy from one to
four, and then you offer them the choice between

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:36.980
cocaine.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.629
When they push a button and they get intravenous
cocaine or banana pellets, they push a different

00:15:40.629 --> 00:15:42.279
button and they get banana pellets.

00:15:42.279 --> 00:15:46.589
And what's what's amazing about this is that
every time you run this study, the top two

00:15:46.589 --> 00:15:47.589
monkeys, the one.

00:15:47.589 --> 00:15:51.060
Those that are on the top of the hierarchy
don't take the cocaine, they take the banana

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.709
parts, the ones who are on the bottom basically
self-medicate.

00:15:53.709 --> 00:15:54.949
Hmm.

00:15:54.949 --> 00:15:59.470
And this happens even if the alpha male in
this group gets reassigned to another group

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:03.249
of four and then ends up on the bottom, all
of a sudden, the exact same thing happens,

00:16:03.249 --> 00:16:04.779
so it doesn't matter about the individual.

00:16:04.779 --> 00:16:09.689
I also looked at corruption in, believe it
or not, bees and wasps.

00:16:09.689 --> 00:16:13.730
And it's an amazing story about systems and
individual behavior.

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:18.950
I won't go into the whole story, but basically
the gist of it is the way the Hive is set

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:23.720
up between different kinds of species of wasps,
whether it's transparent or not, whether it's

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:30.040
easy to detect wrongdoing or not by what are
actually called police wasps or police bees,

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:34.339
that has a massive, massive effect on how
selfish the behavior of the individual wasp

00:16:34.339 --> 00:16:35.759
or bias.

00:16:35.759 --> 00:16:39.899
And I think these lessons that we have from
the animal kingdom are actually quite instructive

00:16:39.899 --> 00:16:44.069
because most of the time when we talk about
these problems, we're talking about them within

00:16:44.069 --> 00:16:46.239
the silos of Democrats and Republicans.

00:16:46.239 --> 00:16:47.239
Yeah.

00:16:47.239 --> 00:16:49.959
And sometimes it's worth stripping that away
and saying, OK, there's some universal lessons

00:16:49.959 --> 00:16:54.290
that we can learn, and we might as well draw
them from wherever we can, even if it's from

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:55.299
an unexpected place.

00:16:55.299 --> 00:16:56.910
Speaker 1: That's super interesting.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:57.910
Yeah.

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:03.259
And and not that long ago, I read Jared Diamond's
book The Third Chimpanzee, which also talks

00:17:03.259 --> 00:17:09.220
a lot about sort of the parts that are analogous
and parts that are not between humans and

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:10.270
chimpanzees.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:14.760
And there's there's a lot of different layers
to that that you don't really see if the focus

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.089
is like American Partizan.

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:17.780
Politics, I guess, is the point that you're
making.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:24.000
Speaker 4: Yeah, I mean, I think that there's
some problems in society that actually can

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:25.130
be solved, right?

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:28.821
I mean, I think we focus on so much stuff
that is the Partizan battle of the day, which

00:17:28.821 --> 00:17:29.890
makes complete sense.

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:34.200
Yeah, but we can actually make systems of
power behave better for all of us.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:38.000
And that requires some outside the box thinking
that might have reference points that aren't

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:39.240
our typical culture wars.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:40.240
Right?

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:44.980
You know, it's hard to get worked up about,
you know, somebody making a point about wasp

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:45.980
corruption, for example.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:50.500
It's not exactly going to rile you up in the
same way.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:52.350
So and so I think we can draw these lessons.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:56.070
The other point that I make, by the way, is
I, as part of the research for this book I

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:58.790
interviewed, I met with lots of really awful
people.

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:04.630
I met with lots of former despots, war criminals,
all sorts around the world.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:08.899
And one of the points that I often make is
you would find it super weird if somebody

00:18:08.899 --> 00:18:14.360
who was an expert on hyenas never encountered
a hyena, never studied one, never went up

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.350
to one and observed it.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:21.580
And yet there's a lot of stuff in politics
where these monsters among us still are living,

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:23.140
breathing and willing to talk to people.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:28.299
So I went to study them to figure out what
makes them tick and what power does to them.

00:18:28.299 --> 00:18:31.559
And I think we need to do more of that because
these problems are preventable.

00:18:31.559 --> 00:18:38.910
But we have to sort of strip away some of
the sort of preconceptions or revulsion that

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:42.460
we have and start to think, OK, we're stuck
with some of these people.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:43.830
How can we make them behave better?

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:44.830
How can we replace them?

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:46.669
How can we make sure that better people follow
them?

00:18:46.669 --> 00:18:51.120
And that requires some sort of outside the
Partizan frame thinking, I think

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:55.540
Speaker 1: the book is corruptible, who gets
power and how it changes us?

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:58.470
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Brian Klaas.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:01.860
Brian, so great talking about this and always
really appreciate your time.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:03.129
Speaker 4: Thanks, David.

00:19:03.129 --> 00:19:03.480
Thanks for having me.

